 ## Overview
-This method parameterizes the 3D Gaussian covariance matrix $\Sigma$ using a unit quaternion $q$ and a 3D scale vector $s$ to ensure the matrix remains positive semi-definite during optimization [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem that directly optimizing the entries of $\Sigma$ can result in non-positive semi-definite matrices, violating physical interpretation and causing numerical instability [S1]. Unlike siblings such as [Cholesky Parameterisation for Gaussian Splats](3120-cholesky-parameterisation-for-gaussian-splats.md), which uses a triangular factorization, this approach explicitly decouples rotation and scale, allowing for efficient gradient derivation and intuitive ellipsoid parameterization [S2, unofficial].
+This method parameterizes the 3D Gaussian covariance matrix $\Sigma$ using a quaternion $q$ and a 3D scale vector $s$ to ensure the matrix remains positive semi-definite during optimization [S1][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that directly optimizing the entries of $\Sigma$ can result in non-positive semi-definite matrices, violating physical interpretation and causing numerical instability [S1]. Unlike siblings such as [Cholesky Parameterisation for Gaussian Splats](3120-cholesky-parameterisation-for-gaussian-splats.md), which uses a triangular factorization, this approach explicitly decouples rotation and scale, allowing for efficient gradient derivation and intuitive ellipsoid parameterization [S2, unofficial].
 
